WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.212
- You've seen the mustard that looks

00:00:01.212 --> 00:00:02.478
like lots of little pebbles, sort of?

00:00:02.478 --> 00:00:03.639
- No!
- We had it at lunch today.

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:05.960
It was literally at lunch today, Kelsey!

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.237
- I had ketchup, obviously!

00:00:10.112 --> 00:00:13.253
- Sweet. Comforting. Reliable.

00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:16.588
Complex. Varied. Challenging.

00:00:16.588 --> 00:00:17.751
Can you love 'em both?

00:00:17.751 --> 00:00:19.324
Of course you can.

00:00:19.324 --> 00:00:21.552
But maybe one's better than the other?

00:00:21.552 --> 00:00:22.609
Ketchup versus mustard.

00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:23.938
It's debatable.

00:00:23.938 --> 00:00:28.938
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:00:29.643 --> 00:00:31.861
- I am 100% Team Ketchup.

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:32.616
- Team Mustard.

00:00:32.616 --> 00:00:33.220
- Ketchup.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:34.021
- I am for mustard.

00:00:34.021 --> 00:00:36.097
- My allegiance lies with ketchup.

00:00:36.097 --> 00:00:38.374
Hi, I'm Keith, and I'm not an idiot.

00:00:38.374 --> 00:00:41.448
(dramatic music)

00:00:41.448 --> 00:00:42.411
- You grow up with ketchup.

00:00:42.411 --> 00:00:43.828
You don't really grow up with mustard.

00:00:43.828 --> 00:00:46.464
Mustard is kind of more of an
acquired taste than ketchup.

00:00:46.464 --> 00:00:49.401
- Yeah, all the best
tastes are acquired tastes.

00:00:49.401 --> 00:00:52.722
- This is from food
historian Andrew F. Smith:

00:00:52.722 --> 00:00:54.706
"Ketchup is a perfect balance of the five

00:00:54.706 --> 00:00:57.284
"fundamental tastes: salty, sweet, sour,

00:00:57.284 --> 00:00:59.140
"bitter, and umami."

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:00.150
You're probably...
- What does umami mean?

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.439
- Umami? I'm not sure.

00:01:01.439 --> 00:01:02.821
It's a food science word.

00:01:02.821 --> 00:01:04.040
- You can put it on anything.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.166
- All kinds of savory things.

00:01:05.166 --> 00:01:06.547
- What can you put mustard on?

00:01:06.547 --> 00:01:08.764
One thing: hot dog!

00:01:08.764 --> 00:01:11.539
- First of all, you can't
put ketchup on everything.

00:01:11.539 --> 00:01:13.002
You don't put ketchup in cereal.

00:01:13.002 --> 00:01:14.023
Let's just be clear.

00:01:14.023 --> 00:01:15.567
Mustard goes on sandwiches.

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:16.728
It goes on pastrami.

00:01:16.728 --> 00:01:18.203
It goes on tons of stuff.

00:01:18.203 --> 00:01:19.363
- Kids love ketchup.

00:01:19.363 --> 00:01:21.105
- Here's why ketchup is for children:

00:01:21.105 --> 00:01:23.521
It helps children eat
foods that they don't like.

00:01:23.521 --> 00:01:25.225
It's just there to cover up flavors

00:01:25.225 --> 00:01:26.806
that they haven't acquired yet.

00:01:26.806 --> 00:01:28.558
- Without this, I wouldn't have made

00:01:28.558 --> 00:01:31.901
movies in my backyard when I was 12,

00:01:31.901 --> 00:01:33.399
where people died.

00:01:33.399 --> 00:01:34.445
- Ketchup looks like blood.

00:01:34.445 --> 00:01:35.861
Mustard looks like pus.

00:01:35.861 --> 00:01:36.963
I have a video that's got
- It looks like pus?

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:38.866
- over three million views on YouTube

00:01:38.866 --> 00:01:41.722
in which I use mustard
to make pus for a pimple.

00:01:41.722 --> 00:01:46.095
- I am very surprised you had
a counter argument for that.

00:01:46.095 --> 00:01:47.731
- I know what ketchup is!

00:01:47.731 --> 00:01:48.927
It's a tomato.

00:01:48.927 --> 00:01:50.587
Da fuq is mustard?

00:01:50.587 --> 00:01:51.656
What is a mustard?

00:01:51.656 --> 00:01:52.630
- (laughing) It's a seed!

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:55.694
(dramatic music)

00:01:55.694 --> 00:01:57.506
- Ketchup is not a category of food.

00:01:57.506 --> 00:01:59.771
Ketchup is just one sad thing

00:01:59.771 --> 00:02:01.535
that's consumed by sad people.

00:02:01.535 --> 00:02:04.286
- Who's eaten more of their
condiment during this battle?

00:02:04.286 --> 00:02:06.028
I've eaten way more mustard than Chris

00:02:06.028 --> 00:02:08.673
has eaten ketchup, because I love mustard,

00:02:08.673 --> 00:02:10.941
(laughing) and he hates ketchup.

00:02:11.761 --> 00:02:13.091
- Mmm! Umami.

00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:14.599
I taste the umami.

00:02:14.599 --> 00:02:15.899
- Now I know your entire character

00:02:15.899 --> 00:02:18.292
here at Buzzfeed is the girl who indulges

00:02:18.292 --> 00:02:19.569
in things that's bad for them,

00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:21.019
and I don't give a shit.

00:02:21.019 --> 00:02:23.458
It's sort of run its
course, I think by now,

00:02:23.458 --> 00:02:25.048
and ketchup is for children.

00:02:25.048 --> 00:02:26.256
It's what you give children because

00:02:26.256 --> 00:02:28.056
it's sugar, salt, and tomato.

00:02:28.056 --> 00:02:29.192
- Delicious things.

00:02:29.192 --> 00:02:30.736
- It's an ancient condiment.

00:02:30.736 --> 00:02:32.188
- Mustard can be traced back to

00:02:32.188 --> 00:02:33.872
Ancient Greece, to France.

00:02:33.872 --> 00:02:35.473
- I think the Romans invented it.

00:02:35.473 --> 00:02:36.750
- Are you sure about that?

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:37.388
- I'm pretty sure.

00:02:37.388 --> 00:02:38.874
- They also ate so much that they would

00:02:38.874 --> 00:02:41.348
throw up, and then eat again, so...

00:02:41.348 --> 00:02:43.576
- That's actually a misconception.

00:02:43.576 --> 00:02:44.354
It's not true.

00:02:44.354 --> 00:02:45.329
- Are you sure about that?

00:02:45.329 --> 00:02:47.395
- Yeah, I made a video about it.

00:02:47.395 --> 00:02:48.127
- Oh my god.

00:02:48.127 --> 00:02:50.076
- You know what's not
on a charcuterie tray?

00:02:50.076 --> 00:02:51.749
Fucking ketchup, because ketchup

00:02:51.749 --> 00:02:53.432
is worthless, and not worth your

00:02:53.432 --> 00:02:55.174
expensive ingredients to dip into.

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:56.172
- Ugh, this sounds like...

00:02:56.172 --> 00:02:57.773
what's that thing where you're racist

00:02:57.773 --> 00:02:59.027
towards class?

00:02:59.027 --> 00:02:59.956
- Classist.

00:02:59.956 --> 00:03:01.988
- That sounds like what mustard is.

00:03:01.988 --> 00:03:05.133
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:06.353
- Andy Warhol once said, "All Cokes

00:03:06.353 --> 00:03:08.791
"are the same, and all Cokes are good."

00:03:08.791 --> 00:03:11.066
And that's kinda how I feel about ketchup.

00:03:11.066 --> 00:03:12.424
Yeah, it's all the same.
- He wasn't talking

00:03:12.424 --> 00:03:14.513
about ketchup, he was talking about Coke!

00:03:14.513 --> 00:03:17.114
- If you want to grow up,
and enjoy actual flavor,

00:03:17.114 --> 00:03:20.794
there's a yellow seed out there for you.

00:03:20.794 --> 00:03:22.548
- Ketchup is a nostalgic food.

00:03:22.548 --> 00:03:23.965
It's from when you were a kid.

00:03:23.965 --> 00:03:25.345
So it gives you nice, warm feelings

00:03:25.345 --> 00:03:26.668
about being a child.

00:03:26.668 --> 00:03:28.248
- So, like, boogers and worms.

00:03:28.248 --> 00:03:33.010
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:35.707
- Ketchup is everything.

00:03:35.707 --> 00:03:36.981
We are all ketchup.

00:03:38.201 --> 00:03:39.323
Somebody in the comments tell me

00:03:39.323 --> 00:03:41.511
what umami is.

00:03:43.466 --> 00:03:45.045
- It's here! It's here.

00:03:45.045 --> 00:03:46.286
- (speaking together) It's here!

00:03:46.286 --> 00:03:48.056
- It's here, it's here, it's here.

00:03:48.056 --> 00:03:49.016
- It's here.
- It's here.

00:03:49.026 --> 00:03:49.586
- Get it.

00:03:49.586 --> 00:03:50.064
- Get it.

00:03:50.064 --> 00:03:50.738
- Get it.

00:03:50.738 --> 00:03:51.179
- There,

00:03:51.179 --> 00:03:51.782
(laughing)

00:03:51.782 --> 00:03:53.047
- I think they got it.

00:03:53.047 --> 00:03:53.767
Get it!

00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:54.556
- Yeah.
- You get it.

00:03:54.556 --> 00:03:56.594
- It's here.

